/// Self-parented rows, ordered among their siblings.
///
/// ─── Four house instances, one of them twice ──────────────────────────────
///
/// notion `Page` and `Block`, portal `Collection`, linear `Issue`; outside the
/// repo lago (three), cal.com (two), discourse's categories. The parent column
/// and the cascade agree everywhere. The ORDER is where they split, and one
/// instance splits with itself: linear `Issue` carries `rank String` AND
/// `position Int`, each with its own index. Two sort orders on one row is
/// two origins of truth, and a reader cannot know which one a drag-and-drop
/// wrote last. That is the row this file exists to prevent.
///
/// ─── Why the rank is a string ─────────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// A fractional index (`a0`, `a0V`, `a1`) lets a row move between two
/// neighbours by writing ONE row — the moved one — where an integer position
/// renumbers every sibling after it. notion, linear and lago all chose the
/// string for that reason, and all three compare it with plain string
/// collation, which is what `@@index([parentId, rank])` orders by. The
/// alphabet and the midpoint arithmetic are a client concern; the schema
/// only promises the column sorts.
///
/// ─── What is deliberately NOT here ────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// **No `parent` relation and no `children` back-relation.** A self-relation
/// names the host model (`parent Page? @relation("PageTree", …)`), which a
/// trait cannot do. The host writes the pair, with `onDelete: Cascade`, which
/// every instance chose; a subtree without its root is nothing.
///
/// **No `depth` and no `path`.** Both are caches of what the parent chain
/// already says, and both go stale on the first move. notion renders a
/// 6-deep tree from the parent column alone. An app that measures a real
/// need for a materialized path declares it as the cache it is, `@system`.
///
/// **No `rootId`.** Same reason, and the same app can add it.
trait Tree {
  /// The identity of the parent row, nullable at the root. The relation that
  /// gives it a type and a cascade is the host's line.
  parentId String?

  /// A fractional index, compared as a string. `@length(1, 64)` is the bound
  /// every instance converged on: long enough that a thousand inserts between
  /// two neighbours do not exhaust it, short enough to index.
  rank     String  @length(1, 64)

  /// "The children of this row, in order" is the only read a tree has.
  @@index([parentId, rank])
}
